#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# cos

#% text
# `cos(x)` gives the cosine of `x`, with `x` in radians.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `cos(q*pi)` is exact for a rational `q` whose denominator is 1, 2, 3, 4 or 6; other exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% item
# A minus sign is dropped: `cos(-x)` is `cos(x)`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `cos` is listable: it applies to each element of a list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
cos(pi/3)
#% output
#= 1/2

#% cell
cos(5pi/6)
#% output
#= (-1/2)*3^(1/2)

#% text
# A machine number:

#% cell
cos(2.0)
#% output
#= -0.4161468365471424

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
cos([0, pi/3, pi])
#% output
#= [1, 1/2, -1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A ball, and 25 digits from an exact argument:

#% cell
cos(1~30)
#% output
#= 0.54030230586813971740093660744

#% cell
n(cos(2), 25)
#% output
#= -0.416146836547142386997568229501

#% text
# The cosine is even:

#% cell
cos(-x)
#% output
#= cos(x)

#% text
# An imaginary argument gives the hyperbolic cosine:

#% cell
n(cos(im))
#% output
#= complex(1.5430806348152437, -0.0)

#% cell
n(cosh(1))
#% output
#= 1.5430806348152437

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The point where the cosine equals its argument:

#% cell
find_root(cos(x) == x, [x, 1])
#% output
#= [x -> 0.7390851332151607]

#% text
# 355/113 is close to `pi`, so the cosine of 355 is close to -1:

#% cell
n(cos(355), 20)
#% output
#= -0.999999999545658980165936

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The double-angle formula:

#% cell
simplify(cos(x)^2 - sin(x)^2)
#% output
#= cos(2*x)

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(cos(x), x)
#% output
#= -sin(x)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Multiples of `pi` with other denominators stay unevaluated:

#% cell
cos(pi/7)
#% output
#= cos((1/7)*pi)

#% text
# Sums in the argument are not expanded:

#% cell
expand(cos(x + y))
#% output
#= cos(x + y)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sin`, `tan`, `acos`, `cosh`, `trig_reduce`, `exp_to_trig`
